6.3 Interpretation of Results

Model A achieved an R² of 0.5890, meaning that it explained approximately 58.9% of the variation in examination scores on the test dataset.

Model B achieved an R² of 0.7696, explaining approximately 77.0% of the variation.

The MAE results showed that Model A had an average absolute prediction error of approximately 1.47 examination-score points, while Model B had an average absolute prediction error of approximately 0.45 points.

Model B also achieved lower MSE and RMSE values.

These results indicate that Model B provided better predictions than Model A on the held-out testing dataset.
